<Layout title="How cppMax runs C++ on iOS" description="The compiler, signing step, interpreter, and virtual Linux layer inside cppMax.">
  <PageHero eyebrow="The technical path" title="A compiler, an ELF, and an interpreter." description="Apple does not allow an app to execute newly compiled native code directly. cppMax takes a different route that Apple permits." />

  <section class="section"><div class="shell premise"><p class="eyebrow">The constraint</p><h2>Clang can create native code on an iPad. iOS will not execute that new code as an app.</h2><p class="lead">cppMax interprets the arm64 instructions instead. The executable is real. The way it runs is different.</p></div></section>

  <section class="pipeline-section"><div class="shell pipeline">
    <article><span>01</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Source</p><h2>You build a normal C++ project.</h2><p>The editor works with local files. Git brings in existing projects, and CMake configures most common builds.</p></div><code>main.cpp<br />parser.cpp<br />CMakeLists.txt</code></article>
    <article><span>02</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Compile</p><h2>Clang and LLD produce an arm64 ELF.</h2><p>The toolchain runs on the device. The output is a standard executable for the arm64 architecture.</p></div><code>clang++ → .o<br />ld.lld → ELF</code></article>
    <article><span>03</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Sign</p><h2>cppMax records the ELF's SHA-256 hash.</h2><p>The signature authorizes that exact build. Change the executable and its hash changes, so the new build needs another signature.</p></div><code>SHA-256<br />6b87...e42a</code></article>
    <article><span>04</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Run</p><h2>The AArch64 interpreter executes each instruction.</h2><p>cppMax reads the executable's arm64 instructions and performs their effects without asking iOS to load the ELF as native app code.</p></div><code>ELF → decode<br />decode → execute</code></article>
    <article><span>05</span><div><p class="eyebrow">System calls</p><h2>A small Linux layer talks to iOS.</h2><p>The beta maps roughly 50 Linux system calls to the matching iOS operations. That is how a C++ program can read or create real files.</p></div><code>open()<br />read()<br />write()</code></article>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="shell repeat-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Why signing is once per build</p><h2>The signature belongs to the file, not the run.</h2></div><div><p>Run the same signed ELF once or a thousand times. It does not use another envToken. Recompile it and you get a different hash, so cppMax signs the new file.</p><a class="text-link" href="/pricing/">See beta and post-beta pricing</a></div></div></section>

  <section class="section rule"><div class="shell honest"><div><span class="tag">Current beta</span><h3>File tools and console programs</h3><p>The current Linux layer covers a focused set of system calls. It is useful for command-line tools and file processing.</p></div><div><span class="tag">Planned</span><h3>OpenGL translated to Metal</h3><p>The v1 plan adds OpenGL support. cppMax will translate graphics work to Metal during execution.</p></div></div></section>
</Layout>
